@page "/docs"

<PageTitle>Docs: DbnSuite</PageTitle>

<h1>Documentation</h1>
<p class="lead">
    DbnSuite is five tools around one archive. DbnSync fills it; DbnFetch and DbnClient read it; DbnTape turns it into
    compact tapes for backtesting; and the optional DbnSpool keeps those tapes up to date in a second store. You can
    run all of them, or only the readers against an archive your team keeps under the same Databento® licence.
</p>

<DatabentoNotice />

<h2 id="how">How the pieces fit</h2>
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li>
        <strong>DbnSync fills an S3 bucket.</strong>
        It runs in Docker: on a DigitalOcean Droplet with a Space as its store, or beside an S3 server on a NAS or home server. Each pass works out which
        days the store is missing, asks Databento for the ones your plan includes at no charge, and stores each file
        exactly as delivered.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The archive is plain S3.</strong>
        One zstd-compressed DBN file per root, schema and UTC day, under predictable keys. Anything that speaks S3
        and reads DBN can use it, without DbnSync running. See <a href="docs/archive">The archive</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>DbnFetch and DbnClient read it.</strong>
        Each person gets a read-only key. <code>dbnf</code> lists the archive, downloads raw files and exports
        processed data; DbnClient does the same from your own C#.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>DbnTape makes backtest tapes, and DbnSpool keeps them current.</strong>
        <code>dbnf tape</code> and DbnClient build each contract's trade date into small S1, Trade and L1 tapes, and
        prove them against the archive. DbnSpool, an optional Docker worker, does the same every hour and uploads each
        new date to a private store of its own.
    </li>
</ol>

